[0.010, "o", "\r\n==============================================================================\r\nQ: Why did signups fall in the week of 15 July 2026?\r\n==============================================================================\r\n\r\nConfidence: low\r\nTook 77s over 5 steps, 24,794 tokens, 16 observations gathered, 11 cited\r\n\r\nANSWER\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  Signups genuinely fell the week of 15 July 2026: mobile conversion rate dropped from 3.50% to 2.90% (-17.4%) while desktop conversion rate stayed flat, and onboarding completions fell from an 82–87/day baseline to 63–67/day starting exactly on 15 July.  [ga4.compare_periods 296fc01a, posthog.event_trend 62cb4db0]\r\n  The most likely cause is commit 91c3e4a, PR #913, 'Rework mobile onboarding modal,' merged and deployed to prod-web on 14 July, which shipped with a known, reviewer-flagged defect making the continue button unreachable on small mobile viewports; desktop was explicitly unaffected by this change.  [git"]
[0.000, "o", "hub.commits b3f2a15f, github.deployment_history 598fd01b, github.pull_request_activity f12caf1f]\r\n  A general-demand explanation was checked and rejected: desktop lost a similar share of sessions but its conversion rate held steady, whereas mobile's conversion rate itself deteriorated, and no commits were found in the native mobile app repo during this period.  [ga4.compare_periods 296fc01a, github.commits b762be41]\r\n\r\nFINDINGS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  Signups fell in the week of 15 July, driven by mobile conversion, not by traffic loss  (high)\r\n    - Mobile conversions fell 31.14% (411→283) week over week, with the mobile session-to-conversion rate dropping from 3.50% to 2.90%.  [ga4.compare_periods 296fc01a]\r\n    - Desktop conversions fell 16.76% (173→144), tracking almost exactly the 16.69% drop in desktop sessions, so desktop's conversion rate barely moved (-0.09%), consistent with a traffic-driven rather than funnel-driven change.  [ga4.compar"]
[0.000, "o", "e_periods 296fc01a]\r\n    - Onboarding-completion events, flat at 82–87 per day from 1–14 July, dropped to a new lower band of 63–67 per day starting 15 July and held there through 21 July.  [posthog.event_trend 62cb4db0]\r\n  A mobile onboarding modal rework (PR #913) shipped the day before the drop began, with a known usability defect left unfixed  (high)\r\n    - Commit 91c3e4a, 'Rework mobile onboarding modal (#913)', merged to acme/web at 2026-07-14T10:00:00Z and was deployed to prod-web at 2026-07-14T11:04:00Z.  [github.commits b3f2a15f, github.deployment_history 598fd01b]\r\n    - PR #913's own description states desktop was unchanged by the rework, matching the finding that only mobile conversion rate moved.  [github.pull_request_activity f12caf1f, ga4.compare_periods 296fc01a]\r\n    - A reviewer flagged before merge that on a 375px viewport with the keyboard open the continue button sits below the fold and is unreachable on an iPhone SE, requested a sticky-footer fix, and the author merged anyway citin"]
[0.000, "o", "g a launch date, shipping the sticky footer 'to follow'.  [github.pull_request_activity f12caf1f]\r\n    - A teammate commented directly on the merge acknowledging it shipped without the footer fix and asked the team to watch mobile signups.  [github.pull_request_activity f12caf1f]\r\n\r\nCHARTS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  onboarding completed over time\r\n    value              |▇██▇▇▇██▇▇█▇█▇▂▂▁▂▂▁▁|  range 63–87\r\n                       |           ^ ^       |\r\n      ^1 Update pricing page copy (#908) (2026-07-12)\r\n      ^2 Rework mobile onboarding modal (#913) (2026-07-14)\r\n      ^3 Bump tailwindcss from 4.1.2 to 4.1.3 (2026-07-14)\r\n                        2026-07-01 … 2026-07-21\r\n\r\n\r\nHYPOTHESES TESTED\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  [supported] The mobile onboarding modal rework in PR #913, merged and deployed 14 July, broke the continue-button flow on small mobil"]
[0.000, "o", "e viewports and caused the mobile conversion-rate drop starting 15 July.\r\n      Timing lines up (deploy 14 Jul, drop starts 15 Jul), the change is mobile-only by the author's own description, the conversion-rate drop is mobile-only, and a known, unfixed viewport defect that blocks the completion button was flagged in review and merged anyway. No other deploy or repo change in the window touches mobile.\r\n  [contradicted] The overall signup drop is explained mainly by a general decline in traffic/demand across channels, not a funnel-specific problem.\r\n      If demand alone explained it, conversion rate would hold roughly steady on both platforms as sessions fell equally (which is what happened on desktop, -0.09% rate change). Instead mobile's conversion rate itself fell 17.36% even though mobile sessions fell by nearly the same percentage as desktop sessions, pointing to a funnel-level problem specific to mobile rather than a traffic-level one.\r\n  [inconclusive] A marketing-site or pricing change (e.g. the pric"]
[0.000, "o", "ing-page copy update on acme/web, commit 77aa21b, 12 July) drove the signup decline.\r\n      This commit predates the 15 July onset by three days and deployed 12 July, two days before the observed effect begins, and it would be expected to affect all traffic, not mobile specifically. Not directly ruled out by a contradicting observation, but the timing and device-specificity make it an unlikely explanation; treated as inconclusive rather than contradicted since no direct test of pricing-page traffic was run.\r\n  [contradicted] A separate code change in the mobile app repo (acme/mobile) caused the drop.\r\n      No commits were found in acme/mobile for the window 8–21 July, ruling out a native app change as the cause; the drop is web-funnel based (GA4 sessionConversionRate on mobile web), not native-app based.\r\n\r\nRECOMMENDATIONS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  P1 Ship the sticky footer fix for the onboarding modal continue button on small mobile viewports (refer"]
[0.000, "o", "enced in PR #913 review) as a priority hotfix, or roll back commit 91c3e4a on acme/web.\r\n      why: The reviewer identified the exact defect before merge (button unreachable on 375px viewport with keyboard open) and it was shipped anyway; this is the most direct, actionable fix tied to the measured mobile conversion-rate drop.\r\n      [github.pull_request_activity f12caf1f, ga4.compare_periods 296fc01a]\r\n  P2 After deploying the fix, re-run the same GA4 device-category comparison for the following week to confirm mobile conversion rate recovers toward its prior ~3.5% baseline.\r\n      why: Confirms the causal link by checking that the effect reverses when the proposed cause is removed, which is stronger evidence than the correlation alone.\r\n      [ga4.compare_periods 296fc01a]\r\n\r\nRISKS AND CAVEATS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - Slack search for 'signup broken' and 'mobile app' after 10 July returned no messages, which could mean no one reported the issue, or"]
[0.000, "o", " that the search terms/channel scope missed relevant discussion; absence of chatter is not strong evidence the defect went unnoticed given the reviewer's own comment anticipating problems.\r\n  - PostHog funnel and GA4 browser-dimension breakdown both returned empty, so the specific step where mobile users drop off (e.g. at the continue button) could not be directly confirmed from event-level funnel data; the conclusion rests on the conversion-rate metric and the PR review comment rather than a funnel breakdown.\r\n  - GA4 channel-group comparison returned no rows, so channel-level mix shifts (e.g. paid vs organic) could not be ruled out as a contributor to the session decline, though this does not affect the conversion-rate finding which is the core of the mobile-specific argument.\r\n  - 2 claim(s) were removed because an independent check found the cited evidence did not support them. Treat the remaining conclusions with corresponding caution.\r\n  - 1 claim(s) go beyond their evidence and are retained with reduce"]
[0.000, "o", "d confidence rather than removed, because the rest of each one holds. What the check found:\r\n- findings[1].claims[0]: The commit timestamp in evidence is 10:02:00Z, not 10:00:00Z as claimed, though deployment time matches exactly.\r\n\r\nDATA QUALITY\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - GA4 sessionDefaultChannelGroup comparison and browser-dimension funnel both returned zero rows for the requested window, and the PostHog funnel query also returned zero rows; these gaps limited the ability to localize the drop-off point or rule out channel-mix shifts, and should be treated as unseen rather than as confirming no effect.\r\n  - 6 of the 11 cited observation(s) returned no results (ga4.compare_periods, ga4.get_funnel, github.commits, posthog.funnel, slack.search_messages). Conclusions resting on them describe what was not found, which is not the same as what does not exist.\r\n\r\nSOURCES\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  604a1"]
